vvi9f5m2CHZ/r\nEY6QcilRGWLDiPX7urI4fysYHoBm2ILzHDJcuJhF8bXL2UFF4CScUwY42AhmlMPs\nsLUnTWCAew1sQUtkbXe/2PqCK4LYLDR0gTeXKXnyH5eHoXX/nC9XxMnD7+BlNLLI\nyAR2NGOPaMLgpsjSPtEh\n=mze8\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-05-05T08:43:51Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/davidarce/devrune-starter-catalog/commit/8b1a5a91336e94ea512491978ac9e5174378d4bd","node_id":"C_kwDORwxuadoAKDhiMWE1YTkxMzM2ZTk0ZWE1MTI0OTE5NzhhYzllNTE3NDM3OGQ0YmQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/davidarce/devrune-starter-catalog/commit/572298c6e2fea33214128bd05183893ddda08224","sha":"572298c6e2fea33214128bd05183893ddda08224","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/davidarce/devrune-starter-catalog/commits/572298c6e2fea33214128bd05183893ddda08224"}],"sha":"8b1a5a91336e94ea512491978ac9e5174378d4bd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/davidarce/devrune-starter-catalog/commits/8b1a5a91336e94ea512491978ac9e5174378d4bd"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/13999850?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/davidarce/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/davidarce/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/davidarce/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/davidarce/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/davidarce","id":13999850,"login":"davidarce","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjEzOTk5ODUw","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/davidarce/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/davidarce/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/davidarce/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/davidarce/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/davidarce/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/davidarce","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/davidarce/devrune-starter-catalog/commits/572298c6e2fea33214128bd05183893ddda08224/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-05-05T08:40:48Z","email":"davidarce2915@gmail.com","name":"David Arce"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-05-05T08:40:48Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(sdd): PRD gate before exploration when scope is unclear (#43)\n\n* feat(sdd): PRD gate before exploration when scope is unclear\n\nWhen a user invokes SDD with a vague brief or against an empty ticket,\nexploration starts on shaky ground — the explorer infers intent from\na thin prompt and the planner inherits the thinness. The implementation\nends up vaguely scoped because the *intent* was never crystallised.\n\nAdd a PRD gate (Step 0b) between active-workflow marker save and the\nexplore phase. The gate is opt-in via one user question; recommended\noption flips based on prompt length and ticket body presence.\n\nComponents:\n\n1. New skill `skills/write-a-prd/`. Generates a PRD via interactive\n   interview. Three rules from grill-me (one question at a time;\n   propose recommended answer for each; explore codebase instead of\n   asking when possible) plus three checkpoints to bound the\n   interview (vague-answer pushback once per branch; periodic stop\n   offer every ~3 rounds; stop on user signal).\n\n   The PRD template is SDD-tailored: Problem Statement / Solution /\n   User Stories (long, \"As X, I want Y, so that Z\") / Out of Scope /\n   Further Notes. Excludes Implementation Decisions, Testing\n   Decisions, file paths, and code snippets — those live in plan.md\n   owned by sdd-plan. The PRD captures intent, not execution.\n\n   The skill writes to `.sdd/{change}/prd.md` when invoked from SDD,\n   or `prd.md` in the current directory otherwise.\n\n2. Step 0b added to all four ORCHESTRATOR variants (base / claude /\n   copilot / opencode) with the appropriate invocation mechanism per\n   variant (`Skill(\"write-a-prd\")` for the three that support it,\n   `@write-a-prd` for copilot).\n\n3. One-line additions to sdd-explore/SKILL.md (Step 1) and\n   sdd-plan/SKILL.md (Step 1) to read `prd.md` when present. Phases\n   degrade gracefully when no PRD exists — behaviour unchanged.\n\nThe skill runs in the orchestrator''s main session context, not in a\nsub-agent. User interaction needs the main session, and a sub-agent\nround-trip per question would cost more tokens than running the\ninterview inline. The skill is sized to ~70 lines plus ~2 lines per\ninterview round, bounded by the user''s stop signal.\n\nInspired by Matt Pocock''s `write-a-prd` skill from the AI engineer\nworkshop 2026 project. Adapted by writing to `.sdd/{change}/prd.md`,\ntrimming Implementation/Testing decisions (delegated to sdd-plan),\nand adding the three checkpoints to bound the interview.\n\nCloses #42.\n\n* refactor(sdd): tighten the PRD gate based on review feedback\n\nThree refinements (mirrored from the equivalent feedback on the\nsister PR in lib-purchaseai):\n\n1. Skill — drop the literal phrase enumeration (\"no sé\" / \"decide\n   tú\" / \"lo que tú digas\") for the vague-answer pushback rule.\n   Replace with a semantic description: \"any reply that doesn''t\n   actually clarify the question, regardless of phrasing\". Vagueness\n   is a property of the answer, not of specific words — the agent\n   shouldn''t be matching strings.\n\n2. Orchestrator — rename \"Step 0b\" to \"Step 1\" across all four\n   variants (base / claude / copilot / opencode). The \"0b\" suffix\n   implied a sibling \"Step 0a\" that doesn''t exist; this is the first\n   numbered step at the orchestrator level, so call it Step 1\n   directly.\n\n3. Orchestrator — invert the gate logic. Previously: ALWAYS ask the\n   user (with recommended option flipping by context quality). Now:\n   the orchestrator assesses context up-front and only prompts when\n   the context is thin. Rich context proceeds straight to explore\n   without friction. The PRD becomes opt-in on poor briefs, not a\n   universal extra question.\n\nSkill description updated to drop the \"Step 0b\" reference.\n\n* refactor(sdd): decouple PRD-gate context check from exploration.md internals\n\nMirrors two refinements from the sister PR review:\n\n1. Decouple from artifacts not yet generated. The previous wording\n   referenced specific sections of `exploration.md` (`## Objective`,\n   `### Task:`) — but the orchestrator runs BEFORE explore, and\n   shouldn''t know the internal structure of an artifact owned by a\n   different phase. Reworded to: \"enough to brief the explore phase\n   on what needs to be built, without making scope-defining\n   assumptions\". Generic, agnostic to phase artifact structure.\n   Applied to all 4 ORCHESTRATOR variants.\n\n2. Disambiguate skill invocation in the Copilot variant. \"@write-a-\n   prd\" was ambiguous — Copilot uses @-mention for both sub-agents\n   and skills. Reworded to \"invoke the skill write-a-prd\"; the\n   Copilot agent has the skill in context and knows the right\n   invocation pattern. The sub-agent invocation `@sdd-explorer`\n   stays unchanged (it IS a sub-agent).\n\n* refactor(sdd): English-only contract text + global language-mirroring rule\n\nMirrors review feedback from the sister PR:\n\n1. Translate the example messages in `write-a-prd/SKILL.md` to\n   English. Skill contracts must be in English; runtime messages\n   adapt to the user''s language via the global rule (below).\n\n2. Add a \"Language Mirroring\" rule at the orchestrator-ambient level\n   in `REGISTRY.md` and `REGISTRY.claude.md`, alongside the SDD\n   Role Invariant. The rule says: present all user-facing output\n   (questions, status, artifact prose) in the same language the\n   user used to initiate the workflow; internal contract fields\n   (envelope keys, file names, command names, code, JSON keys) stay\n   in English. Applies to the orchestrator, all sub-agents, and all\n   skills invoked from the workflow — the ambient rule wins, no\n   need to repeat in each skill.\n\nThe pattern mirrors what `review-pr/SKILL.md` already does at\nper-skill level. Promoting it to ambient-rule level via the registry\ncovers the orchestrator and every phase / skill at once.\n\n* refactor(sdd): per-agent REGISTRY variants + orchestrator playbook cleanup\n\nMirror the lib-purchaseai changes in catalog content where DevRune already\nsupports per-agent variant resolution (no materializer changes required):\n\n- REGISTRY variants per agent: REGISTRY.md (base), REGISTRY.claude.md,\n  REGISTRY.copilot.md (new), REGISTRY.opencode.md (new). Each variant\n  uses agent-native invocation syntax (Task() for Codex/Claude, @-mention\n  for Copilot/OpenCode) and points at the orchestrator delivery channel\n  appropriate for that agent (Skill/agent file/named agent).\n- Drop multi-agent disambiguation from the base REGISTRY.md and\n  REGISTRY.claude.md — the per-agent variants make those notes obsolete.\n- Drop \"SDD Role Invariant\" / \"Language Mirroring\" naming in favour of\n  \"Orchestrator role\" / \"Language Matching\" so the rendered catalog\n  sections read as direct rules rather than namespaced labels.\n- Drop the \"Two-Step Recovery Enforcement\" subsection from the catalog —\n  it''s a sub-agent contract that lives in _shared/persistence-contract.md.\n\nORCHESTRATOR variants (4): strip the \"# SDD Orchestrator (...)\" H1 and\nrename the \"Role Invariant\" H2 to \"Your Role\" so the playbook reads as a\nsingle coherent body. Drop the \"Phase-to-Model Table\" — model routing is\ndeclared in workflow.yaml; surfacing it in the playbook duplicated the\nsource of truth.\n\nskills/write-a-prd/SKILL.md frontmatter: add metadata block (version,\nscope, trigger, auto_invoke) so DevRune materializer recognizes it\nconsistently with the other SDD skills.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(sdd): demote REGISTRY heading levels H2 → H3\n\nDevRune''s root catalog renderer now wraps each workflow with an\n\"## {Name} Workflow\" H2 parent. REGISTRY content is injected as the\nbody of that section, so its top-level sections need to be H3 to nest\ncleanly under the parent.\n\nAll four variants (REGISTRY.md, .claude.md, .copilot.md, .opencode.md)\ndemoted in lockstep. Section content unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(sdd): slim sdd-orchestrator/SKILL.md template to frontmatter only\n\nThe orchestrator playbook now lives in ORCHESTRATOR.{variant}.md and is\nmerged into the rendered SKILL.md at materialize time by DevRune''s\nClaude renderer (RenderOrchestratorSkill). Keeping a \"Read\nORCHESTRATOR.md\" pointer body in the source SKILL.md template would\nduplicate after the merge.\n\nDrop the body so the template carries only the metadata (frontmatter +\nallowed-tools); the renderer fills in the playbook from the resolved\nORCHESTRATOR variant. Add Skill(write-a-prd) to allowed-tools so the\nPRD gate can invoke it without a permission prompt.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(sdd): point active-workflow marker to .sdd/{change}/ workdir\n\nReplace dead orchestrator file path with `.sdd/{change}/` in the\nactive-workflow engram marker. Recovery has always loaded state from\n`.sdd/{change}/state.yaml`; the orchestrator path was only an\ninformational memo and pointed to files that no longer materialize\n(merged into SKILL.md / opencode.json / .github/agents).\n\n* refactor(sdd): replace subjective context check with deterministic scope checklist\n\nStep 1 — PRD gate now counts four concrete signals (specific files/paths,\nout-of-scope statement, bound ticket with body, concrete acceptance\ncriteria) instead of asking the orchestrator to subjectively judge whether\ncontext is \"sufficient\" or \"thin\". Two or more boxes checked → sufficient;\nfewer → THIN, which triggers the PRD prompt.\n\nRemoves LLM-judgment ambiguity from the gate so different models reach the\nsame decision on the same input. Applied across all four ORCHESTRATOR\nvariants (md, claude, copilot, opencode).\n\n* refactor(sdd): add Output Discipline section to registry\n\nPorts the iterated \"Output Discipline\" rule from lib-purchaseai into\nthe catalog REGISTRY variants — the same place where Language Matching\nlives, since both are ambient rules about how the orchestrator\ncommunicates rather than playbook steps.\n\nTells the orchestrator to default to silence + tools: don''t narrate\nphase mechanics, don''t enumerate the scope check, don''t paraphrase\nthe next tool call. Reserve user-facing text for questions, condensed\nphase summaries, and blockers.\n\nApplied across all four REGISTRY variants (md, claude, copilot,\nopencode).\n\n* fix(skills): drop model pin from write-a-prd\n\nRemoving the explicit `model: sonnet` line from the skill frontmatter so\nthe skill inherits the parent session''s model. With the pin in place,\nrunning write-a-prd from an Opus 4.7 1M-context session triggered a\n\"Extra usage required for 1M context\" error because Claude Code tried\nto honor the sub-skill''s Sonnet model under the parent''s 1M tier.\n\nInheriting the parent model keeps the skill usable from any session\nwithout forcing a specific tier.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"8fa692939e28f398703238d4db1fc627a64d8070","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/davidarce/devrune-starter-catalog/git/trees/8fa692939e28f398703238d4db1fc627a64d8070"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/davidarce/devrune-starter-catalog/git/commits/572298c6e2fea33214128bd05183893ddda08224","verification":{"payload":"tree 8fa692939e28f398703238d4db1fc627a64d8070\nparent 61e67654c80c36dff5a4ea8ecbe2998ee7df465a\nauthor David Arce <davidarce2915@gmail.com> 1777970448 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1777970448 +0200\n\nfeat(sdd): PRD gate before exploration when scope is unclear (#43)\n\n* feat(sdd): PRD gate before exploration when scope is unclear\n\nWhen a user invokes SDD with a vague brief or against an empty ticket,\nexploration starts on shaky ground — the explorer infers intent from\na thin prompt and the planner inherits the thinness. The implementation\nends up vaguely scoped because the *intent* was never crystallised.\n\nAdd a PRD gate (Step 0b) between active-workflow marker save and the\nexplore phase. The gate is opt-in via one user question; recommended\noption flips based on prompt length and ticket body presence.\n\nComponents:\n\n1. New skill `skills/write-a-prd/`. Generates a PRD via interactive\n   interview. Three rules from grill-me (one question at a time;\n   propose recommended answer for each; explore codebase instead of\n   asking when possible) plus three checkpoints to bound the\n   interview (vague-answer pushback once per branch; periodic stop\n   offer every ~3 rounds; stop on user signal).\n\n   The PRD template is SDD-tailored: Problem Statement / Solution /\n   User Stories (long, \"As X, I want Y, so that Z\") / Out of Scope /\n   Further Notes. Excludes Implementation Decisions, Testing\n   Decisions, file paths, and code snippets — those live in plan.md\n   owned by sdd-plan. The PRD captures intent, not execution.\n\n   The skill writes to `.sdd/{change}/prd.md` when invoked from SDD,\n   or `prd.md` in the current directory otherwise.\n\n2. Step 0b added to all four ORCHESTRATOR variants (base / claude /\n   copilot / opencode) with the appropriate invocation mechanism per\n   variant (`Skill(\"write-a-prd\")` for the three that support it,\n   `@write-a-prd` for copilot).\n\n3. One-line additions to sdd-explore/SKILL.md (Step 1) and\n   sdd-plan/SKILL.md (Step 1) to read `prd.md` when present. Phases\n   degrade gracefully when no PRD exists — behaviour unchanged.\n\nThe skill runs in the orchestrator''s main session context, not in a\nsub-agent. User interaction needs the main session, and a sub-agent\nround-trip per question would cost more tokens than running the\ninterview inline. The skill is sized to ~70 lines plus ~2 lines per\ninterview round, bounded by the user''s stop signal.\n\nInspired by Matt Pocock''s `write-a-prd` skill from the AI engineer\nworkshop 2026 project. Adapted by writing to `.sdd/{change}/prd.md`,\ntrimming Implementation/Testing decisions (delegated to sdd-plan),\nand adding the three checkpoints to bound the interview.\n\nCloses #42.\n\n* refactor(sdd): tighten the PRD gate based on review feedback\n\nThree refinements (mirrored from the equivalent feedback on the\nsister PR in lib-purchaseai):\n\n1. Skill — drop the literal phrase enumeration (\"no sé\" / \"decide\n   tú\" / \"lo que tú digas\") for the vague-answer pushback rule.\n   Replace with a semantic description: \"any reply that doesn''t\n   actually clarify the question, regardless of phrasing\". Vagueness\n   is a property of the answer, not of specific words — the agent\n   shouldn''t be matching strings.\n\n2. Orchestrator — rename \"Step 0b\" to \"Step 1\" across all four\n   variants (base / claude / copilot / opencode). The \"0b\" suffix\n   implied a sibling \"Step 0a\" that doesn''t exist; this is the first\n   numbered step at the orchestrator level, so call it Step 1\n   directly.\n\n3. Orchestrator — invert the gate logic. Previously: ALWAYS ask the\n   user (with recommended option flipping by context quality). Now:\n   the orchestrator assesses context up-front and only prompts when\n   the context is thin. Rich context proceeds straight to explore\n   without friction. The PRD becomes opt-in on poor briefs, not a\n   universal extra question.\n\nSkill description updated to drop the \"Step 0b\" reference.\n\n* refactor(sdd): decouple PRD-gate context check from exploration.md internals\n\nMirrors two refinements from the sister PR review:\n\n1. Decouple from artifacts not yet generated. The previous wording\n   referenced specific sections of `exploration.md` (`## Objective`,\n   `### Task:`) — but the orchestrator runs BEFORE explore, and\n   shouldn''t know the internal structure of an artifact owned by a\n   different phase. Reworded to: \"enough to brief the explore phase\n   on what needs to be built, without making scope-defining\n   assumptions\". Generic, agnostic to phase artifact structure.\n   Applied to all 4 ORCHESTRATOR variants.\n\n2. Disambiguate skill invocation in the Copilot variant. \"@write-a-\n   prd\" was ambiguous — Copilot uses @-mention for both sub-agents\n   and skills. Reworded to \"invoke the skill write-a-prd\"; the\n   Copilot agent has the skill in context and knows the right\n   invocation pattern. The sub-agent invocation `@sdd-explorer`\n   stays unchanged (it IS a sub-agent).\n\n* refactor(sdd): English-only contract text + global language-mirroring rule\n\nMirrors review feedback from the sister PR:\n\n1. Translate the example messages in `write-a-prd/SKILL.md` to\n   English. Skill contracts must be in English; runtime messages\n   adapt to the user''s language via the global rule (below).\n\n2. Add a \"Language Mirroring\" rule at the orchestrator-ambient level\n   in `REGISTRY.md` and `REGISTRY.claude.md`, alongside the SDD\n   Role Invariant. The rule says: present all user-facing output\n   (questions, status, artifact prose) in the same language the\n   user used to initiate the workflow; internal contract fields\n   (envelope keys, file names, command names, code, JSON keys) stay\n   in English. Applies to the orchestrator, all sub-agents, and all\n   skills invoked from the workflow — the ambient rule wins, no\n   need to repeat in each skill.\n\nThe pattern mirrors what `review-pr/SKILL.md` already does at\nper-skill level. Promoting it to ambient-rule level via the registry\ncovers the orchestrator and every phase / skill at once.\n\n* refactor(sdd): per-agent REGISTRY variants + orchestrator playbook cleanup\n\nMirror the lib-purchaseai changes in catalog content where DevRune already\nsupports per-agent variant resolution (no materializer changes required):\n\n- REGISTRY variants per agent: REGISTRY.md (base), REGISTRY.claude.md,\n  REGISTRY.copilot.md (new), REGISTRY.opencode.md (new). Each variant\n  uses agent-native invocation syntax (Task() for Codex/Claude, @-mention\n  for Copilot/OpenCode) and points at the orchestrator delivery channel\n  appropriate for that agent (Skill/agent file/named agent).\n- Drop multi-agent disambiguation from the base REGISTRY.md and\n  REGISTRY.claude.md — the per-agent variants make those notes obsolete.\n- Drop \"SDD Role Invariant\" / \"Language Mirroring\" naming in favour of\n  \"Orchestrator role\" / \"Language Matching\" so the rendered catalog\n  sections read as direct rules rather than namespaced labels.\n- Drop the \"Two-Step Recovery Enforcement\" subsection from the catalog —\n  it''s a sub-agent contract that lives in _shared/persistence-contract.md.\n\nORCHESTRATOR variants (4): strip the \"# SDD Orchestrator (...)\" H1 and\nrename the \"Role Invariant\" H2 to \"Your Role\" so the playbook reads as a\nsingle coherent body. Drop the \"Phase-to-Model Table\" — model routing is\ndeclared in workflow.yaml; surfacing it in the playbook duplicated the\nsource of truth.\n\nskills/write-a-prd/SKILL.md frontmatter: add metadata block (version,\nscope, trigger, auto_invoke) so DevRune materializer recognizes it\nconsistently with the other SDD skills.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(sdd): demote REGISTRY heading levels H2 → H3\n\nDevRune''s root catalog renderer now wraps each workflow with an\n\"## {Name} Workflow\" H2 parent. REGISTRY content is injected as the\nbody of that section, so its top-level sections need to be H3 to nest\ncleanly under the parent.\n\nAll four variants (REGISTRY.md, .claude.md, .copilot.md, .opencode.md)\ndemoted in lockstep. Section content unchanged.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(sdd): slim sdd-orchestrator/SKILL.md template to frontmatter only\n\nThe orchestrator playbook now lives in ORCHESTRATOR.{variant}.md and is\nmerged into the rendered SKILL.md at materialize time by DevRune''s\nClaude renderer (RenderOrchestratorSkill). Keeping a \"Read\nORCHESTRATOR.md\" pointer body in the source SKILL.md template would\nduplicate after the merge.\n\nDrop the body so the template carries only the metadata (frontmatter +\nallowed-tools); the renderer fills in the playbook from the resolved\nORCHESTRATOR variant. Add Skill(write-a-prd) to allowed-tools so the\nPRD gate can invoke it without a permission prompt.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* refactor(sdd): point active-workflow marker to .sdd/{change}/ workdir\n\nReplace dead orchestrator file path with `.sdd/{change}/` in the\nactive-workflow engram marker. Recovery has always loaded state from\n`.sdd/{change}/state.yaml`; the orchestrator path was only an\ninformational memo and pointed to files that no longer materialize\n(merged into SKILL.md / opencode.json / .github/agents).\n\n* refactor(sdd): replace subjective context check with deterministic scope checklist\n\nStep 1 — PRD gate now counts four concrete signals (specific files/paths,\nout-of-scope statement, bound ticket with body, concrete acceptance\ncriteria) instead of asking the orchestrator to subjectively judge whether\ncontext is \"sufficient\" or \"thin\". Two or more boxes checked → sufficient;\nfewer → THIN, which triggers the PRD prompt.\n\nRemoves LLM-judgment ambiguity from the gate so different models reach the\nsame decision on the same input. Applied across all four ORCHESTRATOR\nvariants (md, claude, copilot, opencode).\n\n* refactor(sdd): add Output Discipline section to registry\n\nPorts the iterated \"Output Discipline\" rule from lib-purchaseai into\nthe catalog REGISTRY variants — the same place where Language Matching\nlives, since both are ambient rules about how the orchestrator\ncommunicates rather than playbook steps.\n\nTells the orchestrator to default to silence + tools: don''t narrate\nphase mechanics, don''t enumerate the scope check, don''t paraphrase\nthe next tool call. Reserve user-facing text for questions, condensed\nphase summaries, and blockers.\n\nApplied across all four REGISTRY variants (md, claude, copilot,\nopencode).\n\n* fix(skills): drop model pin from write-a-prd\n\nRemoving the explicit `model: sonnet` line from the skill frontmatter so\nthe skill inherits the parent session''s model. With the pin in place,\nrunning write-a-prd from an Opus 4.7 1M-context session triggered a\n\"Extra usage required for 1M context\" error because Claude Code tried\nto honor the sub-skill''s Sonnet model under the parent''s 1M tier.\n\nInheriting the parent model keeps the skill usable from any session\nwithout forcing a specific tier.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJp+a0QCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAZ8oQAGPbxYVP5Um+4enBJK9naCzf\n